findings.body:
The new assertion requires both regexes to match the same log message. The two patterns describe mutually exclusive browser messages ("Refused to frame ..." versus "Framing ..."), so the condition is always false. Both `BlockedWebSocketCompressionTests` subclasses (`NoneAncestorWebSocketCompressionTests` and `NoneAncestorWebSocketAppliesPolicyOnCallbackCompressionTests`) will fail deterministically in CI.

The assertion message says "match one of the CSP error patterns", so the intent is OR.

::diff{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerExecutionTests/WebSocketCompressionTests.cs" lines="107-110"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The new `Framing` pattern can't be checked against the repo because the browser produces that text, not the server. Confirm the wording against the Chrome version that CI uses once the assertion is fixed.

Use OR:

::suggestion{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerExecutionTests/WebSocketCompressionTests.cs" lines="107-110"}
Assert.True(
    ParseErrorMessageRegexOld.IsMatch(logs[0].Message) ||
    ParseErrorMessageRegexNew.IsMatch(logs[0].Message),
    $"Expected log message to match one of the CSP error patterns: {ParseErrorMessageRegexOld} or {ParseErrorMessageRegexNew}. Actual: {logs[0].Message}");

The `_receiveTcs` reset was moved above the zero-byte early return in `WebSocketWrapper.ReceiveAsync`. The original code left the completed result in place on a zero-length read, so the payload stayed available for the next read. Now a zero-length read consumes the buffered message, returns its metadata, and copies nothing. Any later read waits for the next message, and the payload is silently dropped.

::diff{file="src/SignalR/server/StackExchangeRedis/test/RedisEndToEnd.cs" lines="406-417"}

The PR does not say why the reorder is needed. The unchanged `sealed` removal is also unexplained. This is a test helper, so the impact is limited to the stateful reconnect test. Still, the helper now behaves differently from a real WebSocket, which can make a test pass or fail for the wrong reason.

The source-build step moves the internal runtime feed from `dotnetbuilds.blob.core.windows.net/internal` to `ci.dot.net/internal`, while still passing the blob container read token as `DotNetRuntimeSourceFeedKey`. The repo already uses `ci.dot.net/internal` in `eng/helix/helix.proj`, so the host is established here. The repo does not show whether this token is accepted by that host.

::diff{file="eng/common/core-templates/steps/source-build.yml" lines="41-46"}

If the token is not accepted, internal source-build fails on the release branch. The token is a read credential, so sending it to a different host is not a new exposure, but the audience change should be confirmed.
